From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:38:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] kernel32/tests: Test mutex recursion, what waits for any and
 all objects take, and wait timeouts.

A mutex its owner acquired a hundred times takes as many releases, and
another thread gets it only when it is free; a wait for any of several
objects takes the first signaled one and leaves the others alone, a
wait for all takes them all or nothing; setting a set event does not
count twice, and releasing a semaphore past its maximum changes
nothing; a positive NtWaitForSingleObject() timeout is an absolute
system time and a negative one an interval; and two threads that hand
a turn to each other through auto-reset events never see it twice.

These come from CrossOver 26.3's (Wine-Staging's esync) tests, without
the todo_wine_if(getenv("WINEESYNC")) and with the timing checks made
lower bounds: GetTickCount() and the system time only move a clock
tick at a time on Windows, which a 5 ms tolerance does not allow for.
They have not been run on Windows yet.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

